{"episodes":{"205317":"Isolated thunderstorms developed over Middle Tennessee during the afternoon and evening of September 3, 2025. While these thunderstorms remained non-severe due to poor lapse rates, other ingredients were enough to still allow for strong winds. These thunderstorms also produced frequent lightning which was the cause for a structure fire in Mt. Juliet.","205346":"A dust devil was observed on video near Gettysburg National Military Park during the afternoon hours of September 3, 2025. No damage or injuries were reported from the dust devil.","205471":"Thunderstorm activity moving east-southeastward across southwestern parts of Central Alabama resulted in a couple areas of wind damage in Sumter and Marengo counties.","205835":"An intense supercell thunderstorm developed over portions of Saline County during the late afternoon hours of September 3rd. This storm weakened as a second supercell thunderstorm developed across portions of McPherson County. This storm moved across portions of McPherson, Harvey, and Sedgwick and produced several reports of large hail up to baseball size. A third supercell storm developed across southwest McPherson County and once again, tracked across Harvey and Sedgwick counties. Large hail up to baseball size and wind gusts in excess of 70 mph were reported. Extensive damage to roofs, trees, siding, and vehicles occurred across McPherson, Harvey, and Sedgwick counties.","205900":"A shortwave trough interacted with a southerly monsoon moisture and an unstable airmass to produce afternoon to early evening thunderstorms.","205998":"Heavy monsoon rains caused flash flooding in a couple of areas.","206266":"Several supercells developed early evening on September 3 along a cold front, producing hail as large as two inches in diameter.","206988":"Strong thunderstorms generated heavy rain and flash flooding across the mountains of Ventura county. Some shallow mud and debris flows across roadways were reported along with some minor flood damage in local campgrounds.","207172":"A strong mid-level wave crashed southeastward toward the Great Lakes and drove a sharp southward moving cold front through the area during the evening and overnight of September 2-3, 2025.  Scattered late afternoon and evening thunderstorms developed within the environment of around 1500 J/kg MUCAPE and 25 knots of deep-layer shear, with the strongest storms producing quarter size hail and some minor wind damage.","207724":"A frontal boundary stalled just south of South Florida associated with a surface low over the Gulf Stream waters allowed showers to develop over the Atlantic waters on the morning of September 3, 2025.","208252":"Derp.","208323":"A surface low and associated surface boundary sank across the state through the morning of September 3rd. Despite little in the way of instability, with MUCAPE only around 100 J/kg and mid level lapse rates under 7 C/km, storms were able to develop along the boundary with 30-35 kts of shear. Storms were fairly weak, however one did organize enough to produce quarter sized hail that was reported in Guthrie County."},"events":[{"begin":"2025-09-03T00:15Z","county":"Charles Mix","deaths":0,"end":"2025-09-03T00:17Z","endLat":43.04,"endLon":-98.19,"episode":207172,"id":1292598,"injuries":0,"lat":43.04,"location":"0.86 NE Dante","lon":-98.19,"magnitude":1.0,"source":"Trained Spotter","state":"SD","type":"Hail","wfo":"FSD"},{"begin":"2025-09-03T00:22Z","county":"Moody","deaths":0,"end":"2025-09-03T00:25Z","endLat":43.99,"endLon":-96.56,"episode":207172,"id":1292599,"injuries":0,"lat":43.99,"location":"2.1 ESE Flandreau Muni Arpt","lon":-96.56,"magnitude":52.0,"magnitudeType":"EG","narrative":"Thunderstorm wind gusts broke a 3-inch diameter tree branch.","source":"Trained Spotter","state":"SD","type":"Thunderstorm Wind","wfo":"FSD"},{"begin":"2025-09-03T00:30Z","county":"Ventura","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2025-09-03T01:00Z","endLat":34.6782,"